# Untitled

DNase-mimetics based on bimetallic hierarchically macroporous MOFs for the efficient inhibition of bacterial biofilm
ABSTRACT The accumulation of pathogenic biofilms poses a serious threat to human health, making their effective destruction and eradication significant, yet greatly challenging. Herein, the bimetallic hierarchically macroporous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) (HMUiO- ${\bf66(Z r/C e)})$ ) with ability to cleave DNA were successfully constructed for the efficient destruction of biofilm and thus inhibition of bacterial growth. By systematically adjusting the feed ratios of $\mathbf{Zr/Ce}$ , their particle size could be minimized to approximately $\mathbf{150\nm}$ and the amount of $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ introduced into HMUiO- ${\bf66}(\bf{Z r/C e})$ could be precisely tailored over a broad molar range from 0 to $69\%$ . The developed HMUiO- ${\bf66}(\bf{Z r/C e})$ feature unique chemical and thermal stabilities as well as abundant exposed Lewis acid sites. Benefitting from their open macroporous structure and accessible active sites, they exhibit exceptional DNase-mimetic activities. The abundant $\mathbf{Zr}\mathbf{-OH}$ sites present in bimetallic MOFs could effectively sequester nucleic acids, while adjacent $\mathbf{Ce-OH}$ moieties form nucleophilic attacks toward phosphorus–oxygen bonds, synergistically amplifying the hydrolysis rate of DNA. Such a unique DNA cleavage ability makes the developed HMUiO-66 $\bf(Z r/C e)$ competent to serve as nanomedicines for cleaving cross-linked extracellular DNA and eradicating bacterial biofilms. On this basis, we designed a biomimetic HMUiO- ${\bf66(Z r/C e)}$ /polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF) film which could discernibly suppress bacterial adherence and colonization, prefiguring their broad application potentials in antimicrobial therapy and medical devices.
Keywords: hierarchically porous MOFs, macro-microporous UiO-66, DNase, bacteria, biofilms
## INTRODUCTION
Bacteria are tiny organisms without nuclei. After their contact with an inert surface, a certain number of bacterioplankton would adhere to the surface of an object and form a biofilm. In biofilms, the self-synthesized extracellular polymers such as polysaccharides, proteins, and extracellular DNA (eDNA) would act as binding agents to hold the microbes together [1]. As a robust framework for protecting bacteria, biofilm-enclosed bacteria are more resistant to the harsh environments and antibacterial agents than planktonic counterparts [2,3]. Thus, the accumulation of pathogenic bacterial biofilms poses a threat to human health, especially in infections associated with tissue and medical devices [4,5]. These infections not only challenge treatment efforts but also tend to recur, leading to elevated morbidity and mortality rates, thereby imposing a substantial healthcare burden [6,7]. The disruption and eradication of bacterial biofilms are critical but remain a significant challenge.
Research has illuminated the pivotal role of eDNA in bacterial biofilm adhesion. Although its negative charge initially exerts a repulsive effect during attachment, eDNA engages with receptors on substrate surfaces when the distance between bacteria and surfaces diminishes to a few nanometers, thereby promoting adhesion [8]. Consequently, eDNA emerges as a promising target for biofilm eradication [9]. Deoxyribonuclease (DNase) is competent to catalyze the hydrolysis of DNA, in which metal ions play a key role in activating P–O bonds in DNA and trigger their cleavage [10–13]. However, natural DNases are not only cost-prohibitive but also susceptible to environmental factors, limiting their practical viability as effective therapeutic agents against biofilms [14–17]. Given the trade-offs associated with natural enzymes, the development of highly efficient DNase mimics, through the simulation of their catalytic mechanisms and molecular structures, holds considerable promise.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are ordered porous crystal materials, formed by the self-assembly of metal ions or clusters with organic ligand units through coordination bonds [18–21]. Their natural porosity benifits the mass diffusion while the abundant exposed metal active sites frequently present certain enzyme-like activity [22–24]. Unfortunately, the traditional MOFs generally possess a microporous structure $(<2~\mathrm{nm})$ , which restricts the free mass diffusion inside the MOFs and only the active sites located on the outer surface are eternally utilized [25,26]. Consequently, hierarchically mesoporous and macroporous MOFs with fully accessible active sites are urgently needed [27,28]. By coupling the Hofmeister effect with the structure-directing properties of triblock copolymers, we successfully introduced uniform mesopores into a series of wellcrystallized hierarchically porous MOFs recently [29]. On the other hand, Ce–OH in Ce-MOFs has been identified to present high catalytic activity for the hydrolysis of phospholipid bonds in biomolecules, thanks to its strong polarization and nucleophilic attack capabilities towards phosphorus–oxygen bonds [30]. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{\zr{-}O H}$ sites in $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs exhibit a strong affinity towards phosphor-bearing phosphate or phosphonate compounds via the formation of $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-O-P}}$ bonds [31]. This might facilitate efficient DNA capture and expedite the removal of cleaved phosphoric group-containing byproducts from active catalytic sites, thereby promoting rapid and sustained DNA hydrolytic reactions. These findings inspire us to design bimetallic active sites in hierarchically macroporous MOFs (HMMOFs), and to explore their potential synergistic catalytic effects for more efficient nuclease activity.
Herein, we engineered hierarchically macroporous bimetallic MOFs by forming UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ $\left(\mathrm{HMUiO-}66(Z r/\mathrm{Ce})\right)$ structures with tunable compositions, resulting in enhanced activity for nucleic acid hydrolysis. While $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs are recognized for their exceptional chemical stability, the direct synthesis of hierarchically macroporous ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ using 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) as a linker remains a challenge according to our previous experience [32]. The integration of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ into Ce-based hierarchically macroporous MOFs could not only overcome this synthetic hurdle but also combine the advantages of two different metal sites. The ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ molar percentage in HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ could be systematically controlled from 0 to $69\%$ via the straightforward adjustment of feed ratio of metallic precursors. The $Z\mathrm{r}$ metallic precursor with smaller lattice units produces MOFs with smaller particle sizes and abundant defects, resulting in the exposure of more Lewis acid sites conducive to catalytic activity. Compared with HMUiO-66 (Ce), HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ offers distinct advantages due to its smaller particle size, enhanced chemical stability, and more efficient and sustained DNA hydrolysis characteristics.
Based on these advantages, HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ was designed not only as a nanomedicine for efficiently cleaving cross-linked eDNA and eradicating the existent biofilms, but also as flexible HMUiO-66 $\mathrm{(Zr/Ce)}$ /polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF) films. The flexible films could inhibit the adhesion and accumulation of bacteria on device surfaces, thereby offering more effective and long-lasting defense against bacterial biofilm formation. Such a more efficient and durable anti-bacterial biofilm effect highlights the importance of bimetallic composition and open macroporous structure of HMMOFs in designing biomimetic DNAse materials. Their competence to inhibit the formation of bacterial biofilms also prefigures their potential for the applicatoins in antibactierial therapy and medical devices.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
In a typical synthesis, the microemulsion was formed with toluene as the oil phase, P123 and F127 as co-stabilizers to guide the formation of hierarchically macroporous structure in MOFs. Meanwhile, the salt ion $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ acts as a mediator to ensure the directed growth of MOFs along the microemulsion interface [33,34]. The different $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ doping levels in bimetallic MOFs could be systematically controlled by the different feed ratios of $\mathrm{ZrO}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}C e(N O_{3})_{6}}$ as well as the addition of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , which can be adjusted in a wide range as represented in Scheme 1. The obtained samples were marked as HMUiO-66 $(\mathrm{Zr}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{y})$ according to the different $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ to ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ feed ratios of $x$ to $y$ in the synthesis process.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns display that the topological structures of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ with different $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ ratios are all similar to that of parent UiO-66(Ce) (Fig. 1a) [35]. Interestingly, with the consecutive incorporation of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ into the bimetallic HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ samples, it could be clearly observed that the peaks assigned to (111) and (002) lattice planes shift to higher angle direction (Fig. 1b), agreeing on the fact that the ionic radius of the introduced $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}~(\mathrm{0}{.}84~\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ is smaller than that of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}\left(0.97\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ [36]. Meanwhile, this result suggests that no separate phases of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ and UiO-66(Ce) have formed in the HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ sample, as no (111) lattice planes corresponding to the simulated phase structures of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ and UiO-66(Ce) are observed at $7.4^{\circ}$ and $7.1^{\circ}$ . On the other hand, owing to the inherent excellent chemical stability of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based UiO-66, HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ exhibits better acid and alkali resistance compared with HMUiO-66(Ce). The structural integrity of the samples was maintained after their immersion in strong acidic $\left(\mathrm{pH1.0}\right)$ and alkaline $\left(\mathrm{pH}12.0\right)$ solutions for 1 and 7 days (Fig. S1). Additionally, the chemical stabilities of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ were also measured after their immersion in various $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperatures $(\mathrm{pH}3.0,7.0,9.0\$ and temperatures of 37, 60, $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ). The XRD results proved that all of the HMUiO$66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ samples possessed excellent structural stability under various $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ and temperatures (Figs S2 and S3).
The real $\mathrm{zr}$ contents in bimetallic HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{y})$ were determined by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) and the results are summarized in Table S1. The maximum actual $Z\mathrm{r}$ doping level in the framework could reach around $69\mathrm{mol\%}$ when the $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ feed ratio was set at 6:4. For $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ feed ratios of 1:9, 2:8, 3:7, 4:6 and 5:5, the real doping amounts of $Z\mathrm{r}$ in the bimetallic MOF were calculated to be about 19, 24, 38, 48 and $59\mathrm{mol\%}$ , respectively. It should be pointed out that the actual doping amount of $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}^{4+}$ in bimetallic HMUiO- $\mathsf{56}(\mathrm{Zr}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{y})$ is slightly higher than the theoretical feeding amounts of $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ precursors (Fig. 1c). The appropriate addition of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ in the synthesis process would inhibit the growth rate of HMUiO-66(Ce), rationalizing the preferred incorporation of $Z\mathrm{r}$ species in bimetallic MOFs and effectively reducing their particle sizes [37]. As a control experiment, when no ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ was applied in the synthesis process, the actual doping amount of $Z\mathrm{r}$ in bimetallic MOFs was similar to the theoretical feeding amounts of $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ precursors (Fig. 1d), which further confirms that the appropriate addition of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ is beneficial for increasing the doping amount of ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ in HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{y})$ .
The morphology evolvement associated with the variation of $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ ratio was investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Upon introducing a small amount of $Z\mathrm{r}$ , the particle size of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr_{1}C e_{9}})$ is determined to be about $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . With the increase of $\mathrm{zr}$ content, their spherical particle sizes become smaller and finally decrease to only around $150~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with a $Z\mathrm{r}$ content up to $69\%$ (Fig. 2). The oriented large channels simultaneously decrease from around 100 to $50\mathrm{nm}$ . When the $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ feed ratios were further enhanced to 7:3 and 8:2, irregular morphologies were evolved (Fig. S4). Previous studies have demonstrated that ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ could facilitate the hydrolysis of $Z\mathrm{r}$ salts and hinder the crystal growth of MOFs, ultimately enhancing the dispersity of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ particles [38]. In line with this, we found that the addition of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ was conducive to the incorporation of more $Z\mathrm{r}$ while modulating the dispersity and reducing particle sizes of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ . In contrast, their particles became larger and tended to aggregate in the synthetic system when no ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ was applied (Fig. S5). Nevertheless, the addition of an excessive amount of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ could lead to a decrease in the particle size of HMUiO-6 $6(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ and induce severe agglomeration (Fig. S6).
A dendritic and open pore structure radiating from the center outward can be observed in the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Fig. 3a–f). As the molar content of $Z\mathrm{r}$ increases, the pore structure gradually transits to a bowl shape. The reduced thickness of pore walls from around 12.5 to $8\mathrm{nm}$ further certifies the successful introduction of more $\mathrm{zr}$ according to a smaller cell structure (Fig. S7). Elemental mapping of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ evidences that C, O, Ce and $Z\mathrm{r}$ are uniformly distributed in the entire skeleton (Fig. 3g), implying the successful co-doping of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}}$ in the framework of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ rather than forming two separate phases of Ce-based and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs. Furthermore, the elemental distributions of $\mathrm{Zr}$ and Ce were measured on the line section across a single HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ nanoparticle (NP) (Fig. 3h). It can be observed that the relative distribution of element content of ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ is highly consistent with that of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ , further indicating the formation of uniform $\mathrm{CeZr}$ heterometallic clusters in the whole $\mathrm{HMUiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ NPs. At low relative pressures $(P/P_{0}=0-$ 0.05), the steep rise in adsorption volume corroborates the presence of abundant micropores in HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ as verified by $\Nu_{2}$ sorption measurements (Fig. S8). For all the MOFs with different $Z\mathrm{r}$ and $\mathrm{Ce}$ feed ratios, typical Type I isotherms were obtained. The specific surface areas and pore volumes of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ increase slightly with the rising Zr content (Table S1).
For $\mathrm{Ce-}\mathrm{Zr}$ bimetallic MOFs, it has been verified that the $Z\mathrm{r}$ and Ce are present in the form of $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ , $\mathbf{\Delta}Z\mathbf{r}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{Ce}}_{6}$ clusters (Fig. 4a). The proportions of those three clusters were determined by the total stoichiometry of the sample [39]. To assess the existing form of metallic clusters, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted. Typically, the XPS spectra for a series of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ affirm the presence of Ce, Zr, $\mathrm{~C~}$ and O (Fig. S9). The $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ XPS analysis of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ reference sample reveals two binding energy peaks at 182.93 and
$185.28\mathrm{~eV}$ (Fig. 4b), corresponding to the $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}~3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ spin states, respectively [40]. As for HMUiO-66(Ce), the Ce 3d XPS spectra show the presence of abundant $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}/\mathrm{Ce^{3+}}$ clusters (Fig. 4c and Table S2). The peaks ascribed to ${\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}}$ are observed at core hole $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ signals with binding energies of 916.71, 906.55, and $902.32\mathrm{eV}$ , along with spin-orbit split $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ signals at 898.76, 888.44, and $885.84\mathrm{eV}$ . Meanwhile, the peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ appear at core hole $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ signals with binding energies of 904.20 and $900.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and spin-orbit split $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ signals at 886.56 and $882.04\mathrm{~eV}$ [41]. As the real doping level of ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ gradually increases from 0 to $69\mathrm{mol\%}$ , the binding energy of $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ shows slight changes (Fig. 4b, Fig. S10 and Table S2). In contrast, the binding energies of Ce $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Ce $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ in HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ exhibit obvious shifts (Fig. 4c, Fig. S11 and Table S2), indicating a significant change in the coordination environment of Ce ions. As ${\mathrm{Ce}}_{6},$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ clusters are cornerstone compositions of $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-}C e}$ bimetallic MOFs, the content variation of Ce would give rise to the great change of its coordination status, accounting for their obvious shift of binding energies. In contrast, for $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ clusters, the $Z\mathrm{r}$ content variation is relatively small, rationalizing the slight changes of $\mathrm{Zr}$ binding energies.
As expected, the thermal stability gradually improves with the increase of $Z\mathrm{r}$ content as demonstrated by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA). The decomposition temperature of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ with the highest $Z\mathrm{r}$ content reaches up to approximately $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 4d). Due to the stronger bond between $\mathbf{\Delta}Z\mathbf{r}_{6}$ clusters and BDC ligands than that of ${\mathrm{Ce}}_{6}$ clusters, bimetallic $\mathrm{HMUiO{-}}66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ samples exhibit higher thermal stability than HMUiO-66(Ce) [42]. Then, the number of linker deficiencies per cluster in HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ was calculated to be approximately 2.8, which was higher than that of HMUiO-66 (Ce) (Figs S12–S14 and Table S3). Furthermore, we used the basic molecule acetonitrile- $\cdot d_{3}$ $(\mathrm{CD}_{3}\mathrm{CN})$ as a probe to compare the intensity of Lewis sites between HMUiO-66(Ce) and $\mathrm{HMUiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ . In the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra (Fig. 4e), the absorbed $\mathrm{CD}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ displays $\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N})$ tensile vibrations in the range of $2200{-}2360~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to Lewis acid sites [43,44]. HMUiO-66(Ce) exhibits two types of acid sites, with strong acid sites at ${\sim}2357~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and weak acid sites at ${\sim}2336\ c m^{-1}$ . Upon incorporating $\mathrm{Zr}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , such peaks shift to lower wavenumbers at $\sim2350$ and $\sim2315\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ .
It is found that peak intensities associated with Lewis acid sites of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ are much stronger than those of HMUiO-66(Ce), confirming our proposal that $Z\mathrm{r}$ doping would induce notably more Lewis acid sites. This implies that HMUiO$66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ might provide more metal active sites for the catalytic hydrolysis of phosphorus–oxygen bonds in DNA.
With a series of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ce})$ samples in hand, we compared the hydrolytic activities of different bimetallic MOFs on DNA. It could be observed that the relative catalytic activities for DNA cleavage progressively increased with the increasing amount of the introduced $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ . The cleavage of DNA over HMUiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ reached a notable $47\%$ enhancement in comparison to that over HMUiO-66(Ce) (Fig. 5a). The kinetic processes of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ and HMUiO-66(Ce) in catalytically cleaving DNA were further analyzed (Fig. 5b and Fig. S15). HMUiO-66(Ce) presents a faster reaction rate for the cleavage of DNA in the initial $^{\textrm{1h}}$ while the rate becomes extremely slow after $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . This might be due to the slow desorption of the cleaved phosphoric group-containing products from the Ce active sites, thereby significantly slowing down the reaction [45,46]. In contrast, HMUiO-66 $\mathrm{(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce_{4})}}$ could continue to cleave DNA even after $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , suggesting that the cleaved phosphoric products can be more efficiently desorbed from the $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ coupled clusters, promoting the ongoing reaction. To verify that the degradation of nucleic acids is indeed caused by MOFs, HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ was taken out after a period of time of DNA cleavage reaction (Fig. 5c and Fig. S16). It was found that DNA cleavage was immediately halted, which further confirmed the key role of HMUiO-66 $\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4})}$ as a heterogeneous catalyst in DNA degradation.
Our previous studies have shown that the $\mathrm{Zr-OH}$ site presents a stronger affinity toward phosphoric group-containing compounds [47], while the $_\mathrm{Ce-OH}$ site possesses stronger hydrolytic activity for the cleavage of phosphorus–oxygen bonds [33]. This motivates us to propose that the $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ clusters of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ might effectuate a synergistic effect, leveraging the unique attributes of both $\mathrm{Zr-OH}$ and $_\mathrm{Ce-OH}$ sites (Fig. 5d). On these $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ clusters of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ , Zr–OH might play a pivotal role in swiftly sequestering nucleic acids, whereas the neighboring $_\mathrm{Ce-OH}$ intensifies the nucleophilic attack towards phosphorus–oxygen bonds, thus amplifying the initial hydrolysis rates. Concurrently, the adjacent $\mathrm{\zr{-}O H}$ sites promote the removal of cleaved phosphoric group-containing products, ensuring the ongoing reaction [48]. To verify our proposal, highresolution $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}$ XPS spectra of DNA adsorbed onto HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ are recorded in Fig. 5e. For the DNA standard, the $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ peaks are observed at $133.80\mathrm{eV}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks at $132.93\mathrm{eV}$ Upon adsorbing DNA onto HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ , such signals shift to 134.29 and $133.20\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. This pronounced shift suggests that the P atoms in the DNA backbone experience a significant degree of electron withdrawal when adsorbed onto HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ [33,49]. Correspondingly, the two peaks of $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ shift to lower binding energies after DNA adsorption (Fig. 5f), indicating that the electrons from the P atoms in DNA have been transferred to $Z\mathrm{r}$ sites. This confirms that the exposed $\mathrm{Zr-OH}$ of $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ cluster is the primary binding site, forming bonds with the phosphate groups of DNA, in agreement with our proposed mechanism. On the other hand, the peaks of Ce 3d for HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ shift towards higher binding energies (Fig. 5g), indicating that the phosphate groups of DNA did not coordinate to the Ce sites. Hence, these adjacent Ce sites on the $\mathrm{CeZr}_{5}$ cluster might provide a bound electrophilic hydroxide or water, serving as a nucleophile for attacking the phosphodiester bonds to make DNA cleavage [50].
Meanwhile, we conducted additional assessments of the cleavage efficiencies of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ towards DNA at different temperatures (Fig. S17a). A significant increase in cleavage yield was observed upon elevating the temperature to $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ indicating that HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ not only functions effectively at physiological temperature but also exhibits high thermal stability, enabling its activity even at elevated temperatures. Moreover, HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ demonstrated nucleaselike catalytic properties over a broad pH range from $\mathrm{pH}3$ to 10, with the most optimal catalytic activity observed at $\mathrm{pH}6$ to 7.4 (Fig. S17b). Such wide-temperature-range and $\mathrm{\pH}$ -resistant DNase-mimetic activities prefigured that HMUiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ might emerge as a promising candidate for a wide range of applications.
Such unique DNA cleavage ability urges us to explore their competence to destruct biofilms and consequently inhibit bacterial growth. We designed two distinct pathways to exemplify their antibacterial potential. The first pathway focuses on destroying established biofilms (Scheme 2a), and the other aims to prevent biofilm formation on the surface of the constructed MOFs films (Scheme 2b). Gram-positive S. aureus was selected to investigate its efficiency in inhibiting bacterial surface adhesion and eliminating the established biofilms. After allowing the biofilm to grow for $12\mathrm{h}$ , we incubated it with different amounts of HMUiO-66(Ce) and $\mathrm{HMUiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce_{4}})$ for another $12\mathrm{{h}}$ and the remaining biofilms were observed using crystal violet staining as well as three-dimensional (3D) confocal microscopy. As expected, the biofilm growth in the control group proceeded undisturbedly. On the contrary, both HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ and HMUiO-66(Ce) are competent to efficiently destroy biofilms. The residual biofilm quantities were reduced in a dose-dependent manner upon their treatment with both MOFs (Fig. 6a, b). The bacterial biofilm was almost completely removed when the concentration of MOFs was increased to $400\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the residual biofilm with the treatment of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ is significantly less than that with the treatment of HMUiO-66(Ce), indicating that the bimetallic sites in MOFs worked synergistically to more effectively inhibit biofilm growth. Quantitative analysis further confirms that HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ presents much stronger destruction ability toward biofilms than HMUiO-66(Ce) (Fig. 6c), consistent with their higher nucleic acid hydrolysis activity. In view of their greatly enhanced destroying ability toward biofilms, the viability of bacteria in the biofilm was assessed using LIVE/DEAD staining (Fig. 6d). Both materials are competent to kill bacteria while HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ is more efficient to induce the lethality of bacteria as demonstrated by much more red dots which stained the dead bacteria. SEM images reveal that an increased dosage of HMMOFs leads to more HMMOF particles adhering to the bacterial surface, causing the bacteria to present a shriveled shape (Fig. 6e). Furthermore, compared with HMUiO-66(Ce), the nanoscale HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ can more effectively penetrate and diffuse into the bacterial biofilm, as verified by a substantial increase in the adhesion of HMUiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ NPs to the bacterial surface. As a result, the bacteria present more severely shriveled shape after being treated with bimetallic HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ NPs, ascribed to their higher DNase-mimetic activities and smaller particle sizes. On the other hand, we conducted direct co-cultivation of HMMOFs with bacteria to investigate their inhibitory effects on bacterial biofilm formation. Interestingly, the bacterial biofilm biomass in the HMUiO-66(Ce)- treated group exhibited effective suppression at $12\mathrm{h}$ ; while there was a certain degree of increase in the bacterial biofilm biomass as the incubation time extended to $24\mathrm{h}$ . In contrast, the HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ -treated group maintained a significantly reduction in bacterial adhesion and much thinner biofilms even after $24\mathrm{h}$ (Figs S18–S20). This indicates that HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ exhibits a more sustained antibacterial biofilm effect over an extended period, consistent with their more sustained DNA hydrolysis characteristics. To test whether such destructive and inhibitory effects are extensible to other bacteria, the same test was applied to Gram-negative $E$ . coli. Obviously, HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ also present remarkably destructive effects on the biofilms formed by $E$ . coli. The quantitative analysis for the average thickness of biofilms markedly indicates that HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ could efficiently inhibit the formation of E. coli. biofilms (Fig. S21). To check the stability of the material during the antibacterial process, we conducted the leakage experiments of the HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ in different environments including water and culture medium. Almost no dissociative $Z\mathrm{r}$ and Ce ions were detected in the supernatant (Table S4), as determined by ICP-OES, evidencing their good chemical stability.
HMUiO-66-embedded PVDF flexible films were further designed to check their antibacterial biofilm performance [51]. We measured the ability of films with different amounts of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ to inhibit biofilm formation (Fig. S22). The quantity of MOFs incorporated into PVDF markedly impacts the membrane’s thickness and integrity, as well as the number of MOFs exposed on the surface of films, thereby significantly affecting its antibacterial efficacy. When the ratio of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}}\right)$ to PVDF was set as 1:1, the thickness of film was about $10.9\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S23a). In this case, there was no obvious inhibitory effect on biofilm growth (Fig. S22a), which could be ascribed to the fact that a large amount of PVDF would cover the surface (Fig. S23d, g). When the ratio of HMUiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})$ to PVDF was stepwise increased to 2:1 and 3:1, the thickness was determined to be about $18.8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S23b) and $30.4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S23c), respectively. In such cases, a large amount of HMUiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4}\right)$ was fully exposed on the surface films (Fig. S23e, f), thereby significantly inhibiting the growth of the biofilm and resulting in a large number of bacterial deaths (Fig. S22b, c). However, an excessively high ratio of MOFs/PVDF would induce cracks in the resultant film (Fig. S23i). Therefore, the ratio of $\mathrm{HMUiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}})$ to PVDF was optimized to be around 2:1 (Fig. S23h), ensuring the production of an intact film with potent antibacterial ability. XRD patterns confirmed that the crystal structures of MOFs were not damaged after their embedding in PVDF (Fig. S24). SEM images show that original morphologies of MOFs were maintained after their embedding in PVDF (Fig. 7b, c, e and f). The water contact angles of the HMUiO-6 $6(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Ce}_{4})/\mathrm{PVDF}$ and HMUiO-66(Ce)/PVDF are determined to be $110^{\circ}$ and $140^{\circ}$ , respectively (Figs S25, S26). Both films showed non-adhesion, indicating their strong resistance to bacterial adhesion. Therefore, bacteria are not easy to gather on the surface to form a biofilm. Confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM) was used to observe the LIVE/DEAD staining images of bacteria on different films where viable bacteria were represented by green staining while red staining indicated dead bacteria (Fig. 7g–i). Notably, the highest bacterial mortality rate was induced by the bimetallic HMUiO-66 $(\mathrm{Zr_{6}C e_{4}})/\mathrm{PVDF}$ film, as evidenced by the predominant presence of red dots (Fig. $\mathbf{7g}{\dot{\mathbf{\Omega}}}$ ), while dead bacteria were also observable in the single metal MOFs film, indicating that HMUiO-66(Ce) could also exerted a certain bactericidal impact (Fig. 7h). The control experiment showed that all bacteria on the PVDF film survived, indicating a negligible impact on bacterial growth (Fig. 7i). Furthermore, SEM images also confirmed that $\mathrm{HMUiO–66(Zr_{6}C e_{4})/P V D F}$ film had a significant inhibitory effect on bacterial growth as demonstrated by the fact that biofilms were sparse and almost non-existent, and S. aureus became rough and damaged (Fig. 7j). In contrast, a small amount of thinner bacterial film was formed on HMUiO-66 (Ce)/PVDF (Fig. 7k), while PVDF itself had no effect on bacterial aggregation and biofilm formation (Fig. 7l). Additionally, this bacterial biofilm inhibitory effect was also extensible and visible upon treating E. coli with MOFs/PVDF (Fig. S27). Such exceptional competence to prevent the establishment of bacterial biofilms and bacterial proliferation of the developed bimetallic HMUiO-66 prefigures their broad application potentials in antimicrobial and protective coatings fields.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, Ce-Zr bimetallic hierarchically macroporous MOFs have been successfully constructed using a microemulsion template directing strategy. By systematically adjusting the feed ratios of $\mathrm{Zr/Ce}$ and the amount of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ dosed, their particle size and the amount of $Z\mathrm{r}$ introduced into bimetallic HMUiO-66 $\mathrm{(Zr/Ce)}$ could be precisely tailored over a broad range. Benefitting from the open macroporous structure and accessible active sites, they exhibit exceptional DNase-mimetic activities. The abundant $\mathrm{Zr-OH}$ sites present in bimetallic MOFs could effectively sequester nucleic acids, while adjacent $_\mathrm{Ce-OH}$ moieties form nucleophilic attacks toward phosphorus–oxygen bonds, synergistically increasing the hydrolysis rate of nucleic acids. Such a unique DNA cleavage ability makes the developed large-pore bimetallic MOFs competent to effectively destruct biofilms and consequently to inhibit bacterial growth, prefiguring their broad application potentials in antimicrobial and
protective coatings fields.
